WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.868
My name's Brent Dicesare,

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:11.710
and I'm half of WD Detailing.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.018
And today we're going to be cleaning

00:00:13.018 --> 00:00:14.384
a super-moldy car

00:00:14.384 --> 00:00:17.520
that has grass growing in it, mold, water,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.700
and all of the above.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.242
It was absolutely disgusting.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.200
First, we put the ozone
machine inside the car.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.041
It basically pumps oxygen

00:00:28.041 --> 00:00:29.910
to the third degree into it,

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:31.076
or ozone,

00:00:31.076 --> 00:00:34.226
and it suffocates the
bacteria and kills it.

00:00:34.226 --> 00:00:35.520
And then we'll pull the seats out

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.070
after we do the ozone treatment,

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.111
so we can actually

00:00:38.111 --> 00:00:40.752
get to all of the spots that we need to.

00:00:40.752 --> 00:00:43.150
And then the first thing
that we'll do is vacuum it,

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.048
try to get all the
debris, or, in this case,

00:00:45.048 --> 00:00:46.410
the mold and the water out of it

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.300
so we can really get in
the car and work on it.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.035
So, here we're working with

00:00:51.035 --> 00:00:53.040
where all of the water ended up going.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.470
The water was only on half of the car,

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.240
and I think that's because of
where the leak was located.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.870
So what you're looking at right now

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.763
is us vacuuming up all of that water.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.882
The last time that this
car was plated was in 2015,

00:01:08.882 --> 00:01:11.810
so it ended up sitting
for the last six years.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:13.520
And just about five months ago,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:14.995
the owner noticed that there was

00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:16.195
an issue with the sunroof

00:01:16.195 --> 00:01:17.710
where it wouldn't close all the way

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.380
and water was continuously
getting in there,

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.730
but we've never seen
anything like this before.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.150
For a car like this,

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.420
it's actually recommended
to remove the carpet,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.420
throw it away, and replace it.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.500
But for some reason, in this specific car,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.170
it was stapled down completely

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:38.460
to the point where if we
were going to remove it,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.760
it wasn't going to go back in

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.680
and sit the way that it would need to.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:42.620
For this car,

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.990
it was definitely a different process

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.140
than when we would normally extract a car,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:49.610
because the carpet was basically stained

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:50.810
with that green mold.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.820
And we had to try to figure out a way

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.000
to push it out of the fibers of the carpet

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.708
and see if the carpet would revive

00:01:56.708 --> 00:01:58.930
to the white color that it ended up being.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.220
So the first thing that we did,

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.660
which we normally would not do,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.130
is use the Tornador attachment,

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.120
which is compressed air
that shoots out in a circle,

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:07.540
like a tornado almost.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:08.960
You have a little compartment

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.535
where you put all-purpose cleaner in

00:02:10.535 --> 00:02:12.480
mixed with water, so you dilute it,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.680
and you attach it to an air
hose on an air compressor.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.560
And it really gets into
the fibers of that cloth

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.630
and kind of blows out anything
that might be in there.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.355
So we blew out the entire carpet with it

00:02:24.355 --> 00:02:26.040
after vacuuming it.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.281
And then we'll go ahead

00:02:27.281 --> 00:02:29.540
and we'll use our extractor soap on it

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.920
and spray a healthy amount on there.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.000
And we'll actually use the extractor,

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
which shoots hot water into the carpet,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.940
to get the carpet a little bit more wet,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.710
because when we use our drill brush,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.890
we want to try to reduce
the amount of friction

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.900
that we're putting into the carpet.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.400
Because if there's too much friction,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.090
if it's not wet enough,

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.678
then it can actually damage the carpet.

00:02:51.678 --> 00:02:55.240
We'll use our drill brush
to agitate all of that soap.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.100
So we'll go over it a few times,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.050
get it really into the fibers.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.030
And then we'll use our
hot-water extractor,

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.030
which only has water in it.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.110
We don't ever recommend
putting any kind of soaps

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:06.150
or anything in your extractor,

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.810
because when you're blowing
the water into the carpet,

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.250
you want to suck everything out,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.750
and if you have soap in it,

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:12.940
it's just going to add more to it

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.890
and it's not going to be completely clean.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.980
I actually filled half of
a Shop-Vac full of water.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.520
So I think I actually got

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:26.373
probably 95% to 99% of the water out.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:29.522
When you do the doors, it's the same thing

00:03:29.522 --> 00:03:31.633
as doing any of the other
plastics in the car.

00:03:31.633 --> 00:03:33.170
In this case, we used Triclean,

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.090
which is made to kill bacteria

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.970
and all that stuff inside of cars.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:40.210
While most all-purpose
cleaners do kill bacteria,

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.040
we wanted something that
is a little bit stronger.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.740
Especially because in this case,

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:45.775
there's a lot more mold

00:03:45.775 --> 00:03:48.680
than you would normally
see in a regular car.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.300
And then use the Tornador attachment

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.280
or some soft-bristle brushes

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.040
and really get in every nook and cranny

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.599
and make sure we clean it up.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:01.900
So, for this part,

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.560
he's actually drying off the door

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.593
and getting all the liquid

00:04:04.593 --> 00:04:05.501
out of the nooks and crannies.

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:07.050
So that is the Tornador attachment,

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.200
but that's a different one

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.710
than the one that is used
for specifically cleaning.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.852
So this Tornador attachment doesn't have

00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:14.910
the canister that holds
any kind of cleaner,

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.690
and this one just blows out air.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:19.540
So you use that to get
into cracks and crevices

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:21.080
where you normally couldn't with a brush

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:23.020
or with a microfiber towel,

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.260
or you use it to get under seats.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:27.368
When we do leather seats

00:04:27.368 --> 00:04:28.901
that have been sitting in mold,

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.190
the first thing we do
with any seat is vacuum it

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:32.640
just to get any kind of debris.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:33.990
Especially in the crevice

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.730
where the bottom and the
back of the seat meet,

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:38.105
we'll use an all-purpose cleaner

00:04:38.105 --> 00:04:39.463
or a leather cleaner.

00:04:39.463 --> 00:04:40.420
They're two different things.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:42.478
A leather cleaner is more suitable

00:04:42.478 --> 00:04:43.690
for high-end leather.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.706
And then he'll spray it on there,

00:04:45.706 --> 00:04:47.322
use a Colourlock brush.

00:04:47.322 --> 00:04:50.430
So it's a really fine, strong brush

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:53.810
to get into all of the pores of the seat.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:54.920
When we're cleaning leather seats,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:56.400
we don't use too much pressure

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.380
just because you don't
want to damage them.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.940
You could leave scratches,
you could tear them.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.780
Spray it again, use the
steamer to kill any bacteria,

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.555
and then wipe that away too.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:09.139
When we do the wheels, the first thing

00:05:09.139 --> 00:05:11.070
we like to do is pressure wash them off

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.479
and get any of that initial dirt

00:05:12.479 --> 00:05:14.070
or grime off of them.

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:15.903
We have some heavy acid that we'll use

00:05:15.903 --> 00:05:16.761
if it's really bad,

00:05:16.761 --> 00:05:19.810
but we don't use it on painted rims.

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:20.643
And then we'll go ahead

00:05:20.643 --> 00:05:22.093
and we'll agitate it with a brush,

00:05:22.093 --> 00:05:23.900
and we'll use a few different brushes

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.260
to really get in both the wheel well,

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.893
on the tire, inside the rim,

00:05:29.893 --> 00:05:31.843
and the drum of the rim.

00:05:31.843 --> 00:05:34.193
And then we'll go ahead
and pressure wash it away.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.090
So, for the exterior,

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:42.000
because it had been
sitting out for so long,

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.530
over time if you don't clean it,

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.480
grime will get attached to the paint

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.080
and start growing mold.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.320
Because we did the
exterior of the car first,

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.250
we weren't too worried
about water getting in,

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.690
since it was already full of water.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:54.531
So we just went ahead

00:05:54.531 --> 00:05:56.564
and we pressure washed everything out.

00:05:56.564 --> 00:05:59.240
We put paint-safe degreaser
all over the paint

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.510
because it just had a really thick layer

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.450
of both mold and grime on it

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:04.525
that wouldn't just come off

00:06:04.525 --> 00:06:06.199
with an initial pressure wash.

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:08.340
We let it sit for about
two to three minutes,

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:10.250
and then we pressure washed it again.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.490
And then we'll go ahead and
we'll use the foam cannon.

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:14.700
So, the foam is just a car soap,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.330
and you just put it into a foam cannon

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.580
and you attach it to your pressure washer.

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.600
It acts as a lubricant for
any dirt that's on the car.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.190
So when you go to wash it,

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:24.250
you're really minimizing

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.130
how much scratching can
happen to the paint.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:27.963
And then we'll go ahead

00:06:27.963 --> 00:06:29.613
and we'll use our microfiber mitts

00:06:29.613 --> 00:06:31.868
to wash it and then spray it off.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:35.180
The absolute best way to store a car

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.320
if it's going to be outside

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.840
is with some sort of car cover or a tarp.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.373
That way, no water is going to get

00:06:40.373 --> 00:06:41.300
on the top of the car

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.030
and find its way into the car.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:44.513
It'll keep the paint clean

00:06:44.513 --> 00:06:46.104
and away from all the elements.

00:06:46.104 --> 00:06:48.100
If it's going to be a car
that you're not driving

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.891
for extended periods of time,

00:06:49.891 --> 00:06:50.800
you still want to look in it

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.810
and make sure nothing's going wrong,

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:54.710
because seals can give.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:57.060
And if they do and
water starts getting in,

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.710
then that's a whole world of problems.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:02.100
So the best way to stop
mold is to be proactive

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.403
and check on your car before
it actually starts growing.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.050
When it comes to detailing,

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.920
I believe it's best to
maybe get a full detail

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.260
once to twice a year, maybe three times.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.900
I always tell people to vacuum it,

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.008
if not every few weeks,
at least once a month.

